{
  "dom_id": "step-q43121",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z=1 \\\\ 6x-2y-z=-14 \\\\ 3x+y-z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $27,$ then $z=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+1z=1 \\\\ 6x+(-2)y+(-1)z=-14 \\\\ 3x+1y+(-1)z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -14 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-2+14)-3(6+42)+(6+6) \\\\  & =24-144+12 \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-108}{27}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
